  "context": "Published in 1884 following Karl Marx's death, this landmark work draws on Lewis H. Morgan's anthropological research into Indigenous societies to demonstrate that social institutions like the monogamous family, private property, and the state are not eternal features of human existence, but historical developments that arose alongside class division.",
  "relatesToToday": "Engels' analysis provides the theoretical foundation for Marxist feminism, demonstrating that female oppression arose with private property and class society. It remains crucial for understanding how family structures evolve alongside economic conditions and why state apparatuses function to preserve class power.",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "The family, private property, and the state are historical products that emerged at specific stages of economic development, not eternal human institutions.",
    "The world-historical defeat of the female sex occurred when private property in land and livestock accumulated, transforming marriage into a tool for paternal inheritance.",
    "The state is an instrument of class rule, arising to manage class antagonisms on behalf of the dominant economic class."
  ],
